example.paragraph:
and the two communication classes are $\left\{ 1\text{,~}2\text{,~}3 \right\}$ and $\left\{ 4\text{,~}5 \right\}$. The matrix $P$ may be written as the partitioned matrix $P = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
        P_{1} & O \\
        O & P_{2}
        \end{array} \right\rbrack$, where

paragraph.math:
$$I = \left\{ \begin{matrix}
        0 & \text{if the event does not happen} \\
        1 & \text{if the event happens}
        \end{matrix} \right.$$

$$\begin{matrix}
        S_{k} & {= S + SQ + SQ^{2} + \cdots + SQ^{k - 1}} \\
         & {= S\left( I + Q + Q^{2} + \cdots + Q^{k - 1} \right)}
        \end{matrix}$$

$$\begin{array}{lll}
        {A = \lim\limits_{k\rightarrow\infty}S_{k}} & = & {\lim\limits_{k\rightarrow\infty}S{\text{(}I + Q + Q^{2} + \cdots + Q^{k - 1}\text{)}}} \\
         & = & {S{\text{(}I + Q + Q^{2} + \cdots\text{)}} = SM}
        \end{array}$$

$$\begin{array}{l}
        \begin{array}{lll}
        {~~~~~~~~~~~0} & {~1} & 2
        \end{array} \\
        {Q = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
        A & O & O \\
        B & A & O \\
        O & B & A
        \end{array} \right\rbrack\begin{array}{l}
        {~0} \\
        {~1} \\
        {~2}
        \end{array}}
        \end{array}$$

content.paragraph:
Theorem 7 can provide this information because the recurrent classes for the chain are just the four absorbing states (at the end of the half-inning). The probabilities needed in Equation (7) are the probabilities of absorption into the four final states of the half-inning given that the initial state of the system is “$0$ bases occupied, $0$ outs.” So the desired probabilities are in column $1$ of the matrix $SM$, where $M$ is the fundamental matrix of the chain and $S = \begin{bmatrix}
        O & O & X
        \end{bmatrix}$ as in Equation (2). The probabilities can be used to calculate $E\lbrack L\rbrack$ using Equation (7), and thus to find $E\lbrack R\rbrack$.
